/**
@mainpage Shoonyakasha C++ technical reference

This reference is generated from the engine's own headers. It includes public
and protected declarations, overloads, default arguments, enums, aliases, and
header source listings. Use the search box to find a symbol by name.

<a href="../../">Documentation home</a> |
<a href="../">API guide and entry points</a>

@section native_start Application development

Begin with Shoonyakasha::Facade::EngineAPI and
Shoonyakasha::Facade::EngineConfig. The facade owns the application and exposes
SceneAPI, InputAPI, PhysicsAPI, EcsAPI, and UIAPI from the
Shoonyakasha::Facade namespace. Keep borrowed API references inside the owning
engine's lifetime. Create scene content from EngineAPI::setOnInit().

For inheritance-based native applications, see Shoonyakasha::ApplicationBase.
The <a href="annotated.html">class index</a>,
<a href="namespaces.html">namespace index</a>, and
<a href="files.html">header index</a> cover the rest of the engine.

@section native_scope Scope and interpretation

Only the repository's include/ headers are parsed. Third-party implementations,
private members, and SHOONYAKASHA_TESTING-only declarations are excluded.
Preprocessing uses NDEBUG, so conditional defaults describe a release build.

Some declarations do not yet have detailed comments; their signatures are
included so the symbol catalog remains complete. An extracted declaration alone
does not establish supported runtime behavior or feature parity. Consult the
task guides and curated C++ and Python contract pages for subsystem limitations.

@section native_contribute Keeping this reference current

Every site build runs Doxygen against the current checkout. Document APIs beside
their declarations with brief descriptions, parameter units and valid ranges,
return values, ownership, lifecycle requirements, and failure behavior. Use
<code>\@param</code>, <code>\@return</code>, <code>\@throws</code>, and
<code>\@see</code> commands where they apply.
*/
